{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/a2-explainer-about-etfs-stocks-bonds-treasuries-liquidity-currency-and-energy-in-investing/audio/main-bf504e1de828.mp3","durationSec":224.64,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":2.74,"text":"A stock is a small ownership interest in a company."},{"start":3.78,"end":6.88,"text":"Imagine that Blue Harbor is a fictional company with 50 shares."},{"start":7.6,"end":11.64,"text":"If Alex buys one share, Alex owns one small part of the company."},{"start":12.5,"end":15.72,"text":"The share represents an interest in the company’s assets and profits."},{"start":16.74,"end":20.88,"text":"Its market value can rise or fall quickly, so profit is never certain."},{"start":21.74,"end":24.46,"text":"Ask three questions: What does Alex own?"},{"start":25.26,"end":26.3,"text":"One company’s stock."},{"start":26.9,"end":27.46,"text":"What changed?"},{"start":27.94,"end":28.54,"text":"The share price."},{"start":29.31,"end":30.34,"text":"What was the result?"},{"start":31.34,"end":33.64,"text":"Alex’s investment gained or lost value."},{"start":34.5,"end":38.44,"text":"An exchange-traded fund, or ETF, collects money from many investors."},{"start":39.22,"end":42.88,"text":"It uses that money for a portfolio of stocks, bonds, or other assets."},{"start":43.78,"end":46.8,"text":"Each ETF share gives part ownership of the whole portfolio."},{"start":47.86,"end":53.08,"text":"For example, an imagined City Basket ETF might hold shares in 50 companies."},{"start":53.86,"end":58.38,"text":"A person can buy one ETF share instead of buying every stock separately."},{"start":59.22,"end":61.94,"text":"ETF shares trade on an exchange during the day."},{"start":62.66,"end":67.66,"text":"Their market price can be a little higher or lower than the value of the assets inside the fund."},{"start":68.72,"end":71.34,"text":"A bond is a loan from an investor to an issuer."},{"start":72.12,"end":75.18,"text":"The issuer may be a company, city, or government."},{"start":75.8,"end":80.72,"text":"It usually pays interest and returns the original amount, called the principal, at maturity."},{"start":81.68,"end":83.54,"text":"Risk depends on the issuer."},{"start":83.54,"end":87.74,"text":"A US Treasury is specifically debt from the US government."},{"start":87.74,"end":90.2,"text":"Treasury Bills have short terms."},{"start":91.26,"end":96.98,"text":"Treasury Notes have terms of 2 to 10 years, and Treasury Bonds can have 20- or 30-year terms."},{"start":98.02,"end":101.3,"text":"Notes and Bonds pay interest every six months."},{"start":102.12,"end":106.8,"text":"Therefore, every Treasury is government debt, but not every bond is a Treasury."},{"start":107.84,"end":113.86,"text":"Liquidity means how easily and quickly an investment can be bought or sold in a secondary market."},{"start":114.2,"end":120.02,"text":"A liquid investment can usually be sold when cash is needed without a large fee or a much worse price."},{"start":121.1,"end":122.24,"text":"Imagine two securities."},{"start":122.24,"end":127.86,"text":"Security A has many ready buyers, so Alex sells it near the expected price."},{"start":128.84,"end":130.78,"text":"Security B has few buyers."},{"start":131.46,"end":133.56,"text":"Alex must wait or accept a lower price."},{"start":134.38,"end":136.04,"text":"Security A has higher liquidity."},{"start":136.9,"end":142.88,"text":"Liquidity matters because an investment may have value on paper but still be difficult to turn into cash."},{"start":142.88,"end":147.48,"text":"Currency can change the result of an international investment."},{"start":148.58,"end":150.7,"text":"Consider a simple dollar and euro example."},{"start":150.7,"end":156.56,"text":"Suppose Alex changes $80 into 80 euros when one dollar equals one euro."},{"start":157.22,"end":159.12,"text":"The investment stays at 80 euros."},{"start":159.78,"end":163.28,"text":"Later, imagine that one euro equals $1.10."},{"start":164.34,"end":168.82,"text":"Alex’s 80 euros are now worth $88 before fees and taxes."},{"start":169.4,"end":173.08,"text":"If one euro instead equals $0.90, it is worth only $72."},{"start":174.04,"end":178.02,"text":"The investment did not change in euros, but its dollar result changed."},{"start":178.52,"end":184.8,"text":"Use the lens again: Alex owned a euro investment, the exchange rate changed, and the dollar value changed."},{"start":185.82,"end":188.86,"text":"Energy can affect investments through business costs and sales."},{"start":189.8,"end":191.98,"text":"Imagine Bright Air uses fuel for its flights."},{"start":192.5,"end":199.78,"text":"If fuel cost for one trip rises from $80 to $95 while ticket income stays the same, its cost rises by $15."},{"start":200.6,"end":202.12,"text":"This may reduce profit."},{"start":203.32,"end":206.16,"text":"Now imagine Sun Field Energy sells fuel."},{"start":207.04,"end":211.68,"text":"A higher selling price may increase its income, although its own costs also matter."},{"start":212.4,"end":218.92,"text":"A stock ETF could hold both companies, so energy changes may affect different parts of its portfolio differently."},{"start":219.36,"end":224.14,"text":"These examples show possible effects; they do not promise any investment result."}],"words":[{"text":"A","start":0,"end":0.18},{"text":"stock","start":0.18,"end":0.5},{"text":"is","start":0.5,"end":0.72},{"text":"a","start":0.72,"end":0.9},{"text":"small","start":0.9,"end":1.18},{"text":"ownership","start":1.18,"end":1.56},{"text":"interest","start":1.56,"end":2.04},{"text":"in","start":2.04,"end":2.28},{"text":"a","start":2.28,"end":2.4},{"text":"company","start":2.4,"end":2.74},{"text":"Imagine","start":3.78,"end":3.92},{"text":"that","start":3.92,"end":4.22},{"text":"Blue","start":4.22,"end":4.44},{"text":"Harbor","start":4.44,"end":4.66},{"text":"is","start":4.66,"end":5},{"text":"a","start":5,"end":5.14},{"text":"fictional","start":5.14,"end":5.44},{"text":"company","start":5.